alue for your first term so this is one  way to to essentially define or to  create a function which generates this  sequence but as we'll see there are  other ways to do it for example let me  do another one and I'll do it another  color let's say I have B of N and let's  say I want to instead of making instead  of saying okay I'm gonna start at N  equals zero and you could kind of view  this as the zero theorem I want to start  at N equals one so what you could do is  is when you input a 1 this essentially  becomes a zero how do I do that well I  just subtract one from it so I could say  six times two to the N minus one power  where n is an integer integer and n is  greater than or equal to 1 notice now  when we put N equals 1 in here and we  could maybe call this the first term we  want to generate a 6 so what happens 1  minus 1 we get that zeroth power that we  want right over there and so 6 times 2  to the 0 is indeed 6 then when n is  equal to 2 it's 6 times 2 to the 2 minus  1 which is just 2 to the first power so  it's just become 6 times 2 which is  equal to 12 so notice these are  different function definitions with  different domains but they're generating  the exact same sequence we could also do  it recursively and we've seen this in  other videos we can define a function  recursively we could say all right look  it looks like each of these each of  these terms in our sequence is twice the  previous term so we could if we want a  recursive definition for the sequence we  can define the first term or in this  case we could say the zeroth term if we  want to start at N equals 0 T of 0 is  two-six and then we could say t of n is  equal to two times T of n minus one T of  n minus one and then this is going to be  this is going to be four or maybe I'll  write it this way  where n is an integer and n is greater  than or equal to zero this would also  generate the sequence when you put N  equals zero here you'll get that term  when you get N equals 1 T of 1 is